# Transport and browser boundary

Floor controls (cookie flags, CSRF on cookie state changes, narrow listeners)
live in `security-baseline`. This rule owns browser-boundary checks and
headers in depth. Session rotation and recovery tokens stay in `security-auth`.
JWT verify constructions stay in `security-crypto`.

## Listeners

- New public listeners use TLS. Prefer TLS 1.2 or newer. Do not add a
  plaintext admin port on a public interface
- Bind admin and debug endpoints to the narrowest interface that still works
- Leave certificate verification on for outbound clients. Disable it only
  when the user explicitly asked to in this turn
- On a new public HTTPS site, set HSTS once TLS is correct for that host

## Browsers

- Session cookies: `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, and the strictest `SameSite` the
  product allows (owned here for the browser check; auth owns rotation)
- Cross-origin configuration lists explicit origins. Do not combine a
  wildcard origin with credentialed requests
- State-changing cookie requests need a cross-site request check the project
  already uses (CSRF token, double-submit, or Origin/Referer check)
- Set `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`. Set a frame ancestors policy and a
  referrer policy when adding a new public site
- Add a content security policy only when the project already has one, or when
  the page is new and the policy is tested. A copied policy that blocks the
  app is not a control — but `nosniff` and frame ancestors on a new public
  site are unconditional
- Do not reflect untrusted input into headers
